WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.627
in september 2026, i had seventeen days before leaving home for college,

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:07.693
two thousand kilometres away.

3
00:00:07.693 --> 00:00:13.034
the plan: build a server at home that runs on its own, survives power cuts,

4
00:00:13.034 --> 00:00:15.313
and lets me leave google behind.

5
00:00:15.313 --> 00:00:18.090
this is how it went, mistakes included.

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.335
the constraints were tight.

7
00:00:22.335 --> 00:00:25.356
no ups, so every power cut is a hard power-off.

8
00:00:25.356 --> 00:00:29.149
a home fibre line with a changing ip and no open web ports.

9
00:00:29.149 --> 00:00:34.419
and once i left, the only help near the box would be my mum, guided over a camera.

10
00:00:34.419 --> 00:00:39.240
so every decision had to be safe to make from two thousand kilometres away.

11
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.967
i picked an intel core i9-14900k for its core count and built-in graphics,

12
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:51.949
thirty-two gigabytes of ram, a one-terabyte nvme drive for the system,

13
00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:55.010
and a two-terabyte nas hard drive for data.

14
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.469
then the orders fell apart.

15
00:00:57.469 --> 00:00:59.894
the motherboard and ram were delayed,

16
00:00:59.894 --> 00:01:04.677
so i cancelled them and bought replacements at a local shop the same day.

17
00:01:04.677 --> 00:01:09.133
a spare board i had turned out to use the wrong socket for this cpu.

18
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.230
the ssd order was cancelled too.

19
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.900
a day before the build, half the parts list had changed.

20
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.824
build day was the second of october.

21
00:01:19.824 --> 00:01:24.267
i checked the socket pins with a torch, set the cpu straight down,

22
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:28.172
and put the ram in the two slots the board marks as first.

23
00:01:28.172 --> 00:01:33.289
i made one wiring mistake: the audio cable went onto the front-panel header.

24
00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:35.780
the photos caught it before power-on.

25
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.420
first power-on: it posted on the first try.

26
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.680
but the cpu sat at ninety degrees doing nothing.

27
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.871
the pump was on a header that never powered it.

28
00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:51.847
i moved it to the cpu fan header at full speed, and idle dropped to thirty-four degrees.

29
00:01:51.847 --> 00:01:54.360
then a full memory test: zero errors.

30
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.079
stress testing showed the real limit wasn't the cpu.

31
00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:02.341
it was the motherboard's power stage.

32
00:02:02.341 --> 00:02:07.598
at a hundred and eighty watts it hit a hundred and five degrees in fifty-five seconds.

33
00:02:07.598 --> 00:02:10.471
so i capped the chip at ninety watts sustained,

34
00:02:10.471 --> 00:02:13.956
and zip-tied a spare case fan right over the power stage.

35
00:02:13.956 --> 00:02:17.440
thirty minutes at full load: flat at sixty-seven degrees.

36
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.836
i installed proxmox ve on the nvme, turned off password logins,

37
00:02:23.836 --> 00:02:27.773
and set up tailscale for private admin access from any network.

38
00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:32.896
my isp blocks inbound web ports, so public apps leave through a cloudflare tunnel,

39
00:02:32.896 --> 00:02:36.270
with cloudflare access in front of anything sensitive.

40
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.214
the rule is: always two independent ways in.

41
00:02:40.214 --> 00:02:44.305
tailscale first, a cloudflare-protected ssh path second,

42
00:02:44.305 --> 00:02:47.811
and later a hardened direct ssh port as a third.

43
00:02:47.811 --> 00:02:56.650
any risky network change runs behind a ten-minute dead-man switch that undoes itself unless i confirm i can still log in.

44
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.623
with no ups, a power cut has to be a normal event.

45
00:03:02.623 --> 00:03:05.772
the bios powers the box on when mains returns.

46
00:03:05.772 --> 00:03:08.989
the kernel reboots itself on a panic or a hang,

47
00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:13.234
because this board's hardware watchdog turned out not to work.

48
00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:17.752
every disk is ext4 with safe caching, every database has fsync on,

49
00:03:17.752 --> 00:03:22.270
and a small guard restarts tailscale if it gets stuck during boot.

50
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.825
cold boots still took up to thirteen minutes.

51
00:03:25.825 --> 00:03:30.723
the kernel log showed why: every container disk had multi-mount protection on,

52
00:03:30.723 --> 00:03:35.934
which waits about forty-two seconds after an unclean stop, one container at a time.

53
00:03:35.934 --> 00:03:40.454
i turned it off, reordered the boot, and pulled the plug twice in a row,

54
00:03:40.454 --> 00:03:42.338
once in the middle of booting.

55
00:03:42.338 --> 00:03:45.540
twenty-two guests came back in fifty-three seconds.

56
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.769
then the part i'd been waiting for: leaving google.

57
00:03:51.769 --> 00:03:58.421
my photos went into immich: three thousand four hundred and seventy-one photos and videos,

58
00:03:58.421 --> 00:03:59.604
albums included.

59
00:03:59.604 --> 00:04:03.669
drive, notes and an old hard drive went into nextcloud:

60
00:04:03.669 --> 00:04:09.286
a hundred and sixty-six thousand files, a hundred and forty-seven gigabytes.

61
00:04:09.286 --> 00:04:11.577
i checksum-verified every file.

62
00:04:11.577 --> 00:04:12.760
zero mismatches.

63
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.961
data only counts once a restore works.

64
00:04:15.961 --> 00:04:21.788
every night the guests are backed up to the hard drive, and photos and files get a second,

65
00:04:21.788 --> 00:04:23.731
deduplicated copy with restic.

66
00:04:23.731 --> 00:04:29.752
at eight every morning a check pushes an alert to my phone if any backup is older than a day.

67
00:04:29.752 --> 00:04:32.990
and i have tested restores, not just assumed them.

68
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:40.806
today the box runs about thirty guests: photos, files, a password manager,

69
00:04:40.806 --> 00:04:46.973
a document archive with tamil and hindi ocr, media with live tv, a private git server,

70
00:04:46.973 --> 00:04:51.131
automations, speed tests and a code editor in the browser.

71
00:04:51.131 --> 00:04:54.860
each one goes through the same crash-safe checklist.

72
00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.948
i can't walk over and look at it, so it watches itself.

73
00:05:00.948 --> 00:05:06.690
prometheus and grafana track temperatures and power, loki collects logs from every guest,

74
00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:08.754
and uptime kuma checks each app.

75
00:05:08.754 --> 00:05:11.657
but a dead server can't report its own death,

76
00:05:11.657 --> 00:05:17.593
so a small aws lambda checks it from the outside every minute and runs a public status page.

77
00:05:17.593 --> 00:05:21.270
a power cut now sends exactly two pushes: down, and back.

78
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.786
then i started building for other people.

79
00:05:26.786 --> 00:05:33.339
api dot dharun dot dev serves free, open apis for indian developers: pincodes, ifsc codes,

80
00:05:33.339 --> 00:05:34.650
panchang and more.

81
00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.073
every push to main runs ci and ships a release.

82
00:05:38.073 --> 00:05:43.680
the server pulls it, health-checks it, and rolls back on its own if it fails.

83
00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.032
i also built a honeynet: a virtual machine on its own sealed network,

84
00:05:49.032 --> 00:05:52.589
running eleven honeypots, an ai-generated fake shell,

85
00:05:52.589 --> 00:05:57.355
and a real operating system behind a proxy that resets every six hours.

86
00:05:57.355 --> 00:06:02.390
attack logs flow into grafana, and repeat attackers get banned at the edge.

87
00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:05.947
plenty broke.

88
00:06:05.947 --> 00:06:12.877
my ai assistant once deleted the server's dev null with a stray command, and ssh died.

89
00:06:12.877 --> 00:06:16.099
now a guard blocks destructive commands,

90
00:06:16.099 --> 00:06:20.370
and a timer recreates the file within thirty seconds.

91
00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:25.687
turning on the firewall silently broke tailscale access to guests.

92
00:06:25.687 --> 00:06:28.427
a power cut left a dns file empty.

93
00:06:28.427 --> 00:06:33.342
and the isp router quietly dropped its port-forwarding rules.

94
00:06:33.342 --> 00:06:35.920
every one got a fix, and a test.

95
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.569
i ran the whole project with claude code as a pair sysadmin,

96
00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:43.611
in a private repo that is the source of truth.

97
00:06:43.611 --> 00:06:47.051
every change gets a changelog entry with a rollback.

98
00:06:47.051 --> 00:06:49.366
risky actions need my explicit yes.

99
00:06:49.366 --> 00:06:52.872
and every mistake becomes one line in a lessons file.

100
00:06:52.872 --> 00:06:55.650
there are a hundred and fifty of them now.

101
00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.395
on the twenty-first of october i was in my hostel in punjab,

102
00:07:02.395 --> 00:07:04.983
two thousand kilometres from the box.

103
00:07:04.983 --> 00:07:09.737
the drill passed: every app and every admin path, from hostel wi-fi.

104
00:07:09.737 --> 00:07:13.933
in the week around the move it took several real power cuts,

105
00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:16.450
and came back on its own every time.

106
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.596
one box, built in a month, that runs without me.

107
00:07:20.596 --> 00:07:26.610
i'm dharun ashokkumar, a computer science student who likes systems that fail safely.
